</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Extended details for MLR and IFA.</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MLR.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the lifelong learning stage, we utilize the proposed Multimodal Latent Replay (MLR). Instead of storing raw sensory data, a compact buffer <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> maintains the multimodal latent representations (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math>), which are concatenated from the frozen encoders, alongside the associated action (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>) for training. In subsequent tasks, the stored <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> from previous tasks is fed into the temporal decoder for replay.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">IFA.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent interference (catastrophic forgetting) between the current task (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the previously learned tasks (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>), we introduce the IFA. IFA’s core mechanism encourages the global latent representation of the current task (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}(T_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}(T_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>) to remain closer to its own stable reference (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(r)}(T_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(r)}(T_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>) than to the references of previously learned tasks (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(r)}(T_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(r)}(T_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>).The IFA loss is explicitly defined to penalize violations of this distance constraint:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{IFA}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}|}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}(j,k)\in\mathcal{P}\\
j&lt;k\end{subarray}}\max\Big(0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>IFA</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mi>max</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{IFA}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}|}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}(j,k)\in\mathcal{P}\\
j&lt;k\end{subarray}}\max\Big(0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{k})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{k})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle}{\displaystyle-d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big)+\delta\Big)\&gt;," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.220em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle}{\displaystyle-d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big)+\delta\Big)\&gt;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The margin <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> adapts to the relative positions of the task references themselves:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} \delta&amp;=\alpha\,d\!\left(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),\,h^{(r)}(T_{j})\right)\end{aligned}\&gt;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>δ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.350em;" width="0.350em"><mi>d</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0.220em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} \delta&amp;=\alpha\,d\!\left(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),\,h^{(r)}(T_{j})\right)\end{aligned}\&gt;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Angle-based distance.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The IFA loss is explicitly driven by the difference in distance between the global latent representation <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}(T_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}(T_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and the task references: the positive reference <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(r)}(T_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(r)}(T_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> (for the current task <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the negative references <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(r)}(T_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(r)}(T_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> (for the previous tasks <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, we compared how the standard cosine similarity and its angular counterpart (distance <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\cdot,\cdot)=\arccos(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\cdot,\cdot)=\arccos(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>) behave when used inside this crucial distance difference.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C.1 Selection of a stable task reference ‣ Appendix C Extended Analysis ‣ Lifelong Imitation Learning with Multimodal Latent Replay and Incremental Adjustment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates this comparison.
The left panel plots <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of the angle <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in[0,\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in[0,\pi]</annotation></semantics></math> between two unit vectors, while the right panel shows <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arccos(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arccos(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Cosine (left) quickly saturates in the high-similarity regime: when two representations are already very close, even noticeable angular changes produce almost no change in <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, making the resulting distance difference extremely small and hard to distinguish.
In contrast, the angular distance <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arccos(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arccos(x)</annotation></semantics></math> (right) expands this high-similarity region; small changes in <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> translate into clearly separable angular differences, providing more stable resolution across similarity levels and allowing IFA to better capture small deviations between task-level representations. Outside this range, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arccos(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arccos(x)</annotation></semantics></math> behaves similarly to cosine similarity—differences between representations remain distinguishable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> as a fixed hyperparameter would incur in a risk of choosing an overly large margin. Specifically, if <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is set larger than the distance between the two task references, i.e., <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;d\big(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;d\big(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, the IFA loss will always enforce a penalty.
This is guaranteed by the triangle inequality property of spherical geometry, which states that the difference between the distances to the two references is bounded by the distance between the references themselves:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.Ex1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex1X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{k})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{k})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-d\big(g_{t}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex1Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq d\big(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq d\big(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds this maximum possible difference, the loss term inside the <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> will always be positive. This would enforce an unnecessary repulsive force, even when the current global latent representation <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}(T_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}(T_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is already sufficiently closer to its own reference <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(r)}(T_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(r)}(T_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, potentially destabilizing learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid this unnecessary penalty and allow the margin to adapt to the distance between references, we define <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> dynamically:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\alpha\,d\big(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big),\quad\alpha\in(0,1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\alpha\,d\big(h^{(r)}(T_{k}),h^{(r)}(T_{j})\big),\quad\alpha\in(0,1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This guarantees that the margin is always bounded by the inter-reference distance, and scales proportionally to how far apart the tasks are in the angular space.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Computational efficiency</h2>

<figure id="A4.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 11</span>: </span><span id="A4.T11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Runtime and FLOPs comparison. See text for the explanation of Forward Time,
S-FLOPs, and T-FLOPs. All experiments are conducted on <span id="A4.T11.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LIBERO-GOAL</span>. <math id="A4.T11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> means the probability of storing features in the buffer, which is ablated in Tab. <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.1.2 Ablation Study ‣ 4.1 Results ‣ 4 Experimental Analysis ‣ Lifelong Imitation Learning with Multimodal Latent Replay and Incremental Adjustment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. RR stands for raw replay, which stores past samples and replays them during training. Note that BASE stands for running our method without MLR or IFA. It is included solely to quantify the computational cost of the base architecture.</span></figcaption>
<div id="A4.T11.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:119.9pt;vertical-align:-56.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(71.0pt,-17.1pt) scale(1.39993648215709,1.39993648215709) ;">
